1021: #      between (D12);
1022: #   2. judges EVERY recovery record of the task first: an active one refuses the panel, the
1023: #      inactive ones are consumed (numbering skips past them; D5);
1024: #   3. assigns n and NN to every member up front, in roster order (member k: n0 + k - 1,
1025: #      NN0 + k - 1), so the files and the ledger keep the roster order whatever finishes first;
1026: #   4. writes every member's `reserved` record (the panel, n, NN, this process as writer and
1027: #      parent) BEFORE it launches any member - a member proves its parent with it, rewrites it
1028: #      with its own pid and only then checks that the parent lives (D6, D1, F07-1);
1029: #   5. launches the members as processes of their own (Start-Process; console output to files
1030: #      in a temp directory) along the plan of Get-PanelPlan - members of different endpoints at
1031: #      once, of one endpoint one after another unless the roster's "parallel" raises it,
1032: #      -PanelConcurrency caps the total (D8) - polls them, prints one line per finished member
1033: #      and stops a member that outlives its guard (its timeout + its format-repair and
1034: #      denial-retry budgets + 60 s write lock + 120 s, D11);
1035: #   6. prints every member's console output in roster order, the summary with the panel's wall
1036: #      clock, and removes the records of members that never started anything (D5).
1037: # A member that fails or is refused does not stop the others; with -PanelConcurrency 1 a member
1038: # that leaves surviving processes stops the rest (recorded as skipped, exit 1; F15-3). Exit 0
1039: # only when every member produced a usable reply (-DryRun: when every member's plan could be
1040: # made; a dry run writes nothing and takes no lock).
